# G117 — Stream Video from Online

*v2. See `stream-player.md` for prose: ABR as pure function, 5-state FSM,
buffer-budget clamping with floor + ceiling, seek invalidates buffer.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Video Library

[video-library @manifest-url
    IN ORDER TO
        play a segmented video with adaptive bitrate
        reacting to observed bandwidth and buffer health

    @stream <- [hls[parse-manifest {url: @manifest-url}]]
    @player <- [sp[new]]
    [sp[attach {player: @player, stream: @stream}]]

    until @ended [
        at @every 1000 ms
            [sp[set-bandwidth {player: @player, bps: @network:measured-bps}]]
            until @buffer-full
                [sp[load-next-segment {player: @player}]]
                    || -> @end-of-stream
            [sp[tick {player: @player, ms: 1000}]]
            #video
                state: @player:state
                bitrate: @player:current-bitrate
                played: @player:played-ms

        sync @user-events {
            scrub @segment-idx -> [sp[seek-to-segment
                                       {player: @player,
                                        segment-idx: @segment-idx}]]
            pause             -> [sp[pause {player: @player}]]
            play              -> [sp[play  {player: @player}]]
        }
    ]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[sp
    new
    attach             {player, stream}
    set-bandwidth      {player, bps}
    load-next-segment  {player}                 ;; || end-of-stream
    tick               {player, ms}
    pause              {player}
    play               {player}
    seek-to-segment    {player, segment-idx}    ;; || out-of-range

    pick-bitrate       {stream, buffer-ms, bandwidth-bps, segment}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: ABR as `where`-expressed contract

The v1 spec baked the bitrate-selection formula into a method. In
Innate the contract is expressible as constraints the resolver
satisfies:

[sp[pick-bitrate {stream: @s, buffer-ms: @b, bandwidth-bps: @bw, segment: @seg}
    where
        effective bandwidth is bandwidth × safety-factor (typically 0.85)
        and budget is max(buffer-ms, segment duration), capped at 2× target-buffer
        and the chosen bitrate is the highest whose download fits the budget
]

---

## End-of-stream as fulfillment fallthrough

The `load-next-segment` call either succeeds (returning the segment)
or resists with "no more segments." The `||` turns this into a
natural loop exit:

until @buffer-full
    [sp[load-next-segment {player: @player}]]
        || -> @end-of-stream

---

## Demo

[demo
    @stream <- [hls[parse-manifest
                     {bitrates-bps: [500000, 1000000, 2000000, 4000000],
                      segments: [5 segments of 2000ms each],
                      safety-factor: 0.85,
                      target-buffer-ms: 6000}]]
    @p <- [sp[new]]
    [sp[attach {player: @p, stream: @stream}]]
    [sp[set-bandwidth {player: @p, bps: 10000000}]]

    [sp[load-next-segment {player: @p}]]
    [sp[load-next-segment {player: @p}]]
    [sp[load-next-segment {player: @p}]]

    -> @p:state              ;; -> "playing" (buffer reached target)
    -> @p:current-bitrate    ;; -> 4000000  (highest that fits)

    [sp[tick {player: @p, ms: 6000}]]
    -> @p:state              ;; -> "buffering" (buffer drained)
]
